  {/* tabindex="-1" IS WHAT MAKES THE SKIP LINK WORK (2026-08-06).
      It was a plain div, and a plain div is not focusable, so activating the
      skip link moved the browser's scroll position and nothing else: on iOS
      VoiceOver and on Windows virtual-cursor readers the cursor stayed up in
      the header and the next swipe read the brand again. The link looked like
      it worked and did not do the one thing it exists for.
      The page's own <main class="wrap"> is NOT the target on purpose: this
      wrapper also holds the translation and language-undo strips, which render
      ABOVE the page's main and are exactly the kind of thing a skipping reader
      should land before rather than past. One target, and it is the top of the
      content region.
      The focus ring is suppressed because this box is the whole page: a 2px
      red outline around every pixel of content is not a focus indicator, it is
      a border. Nothing is lost, because the very next Tab lands on a real
      control that draws its own ring. */}
  <div id="main" tabindex="-1">
    {/* Sits ABOVE the page's own <main class="wrap">, so it needs the site
        container itself or it would run edge to edge. Bottom padding only:
        the page below brings its own --page-top. */}
    {/* ORDER MATTERS. The undo line goes ABOVE the translation notice: it is
        about the page the visitor is looking at rather than about how it was
        written, and it is the one they may need to act on. In practice only one
        of the two is ever visible, because LangUndo hides the notice on the
        pageview it reveals itself (measured; see the comment in LangUndo). The
        order is still correct here so that if that priority is ever relaxed,
        nothing has to be rearranged.

        THE WRAPPER IS CONDITIONAL, and that is not tidiness. It relied on
        `.tx-wrap:empty` to collapse itself where no strip renders, and :empty
        counts whitespace TEXT NODES: Astro leaves a newline where a top-level
        `{cond && ...}` renders nothing, so this div has never once been empty.
        The selector never matched, and every English page plus every pt/es
        rescue-tunnel page was carrying a dead 24px box under the nav. Removing
        it brings the last homepage door up 21px on the tightest phone in the
        gate and the fifth /emergency door up 24px in all three languages. Full
        measurements in the anyNotice() comment in src/i18n/registry.js.

        anyNotice() IS ONLY HALF OF IT, and the first version of this note said
        otherwise (2026-08-06). This test is answered at BUILD time, so it can
        only remove the wrapper where no strip could EVER draw. On an ordinary pt
        or es page one can, so the wrapper is emitted, and both strips inside it
        ship hidden and are revealed by script. Once a reader dismisses the
        translation notice, every pt and es page they open again had the dead box
        back, measured at 24px. The runtime half is a :has() rule on .tx-wrap in
        TranslationNotice.astro that drops the padding while no child strip is
        visible. Neither mechanism is sufficient alone. Read both before changing
        either. `.tx-wrap:empty` stays in the CSS as a backstop that has never
        matched. */}
    {anyNotice(lang, path) && (
      <div class="tx-wrap">
        <LangUndo lang={lang} path={path} />
        <TranslationNotice lang={lang} path={path} />
      </div>
    )}
